1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,976
Gate’s BillingClient expects GET
/tariffs/quote to return { "amount":
"108.00", "currency": "USD" }. Billing
renames amount to total on a Friday.
Gate’s Feign decoder fails on Monday’s
shift change.

2
00:00:15,096 --> 00:00:27,551
Neither side’s unit tests lied — they
never shared a wire contract. Contract
testing makes that share explicit: a pact
or Spring Cloud Contract stub that billing
must satisfy and gate can consume in CI.

3
00:00:27,671 --> 00:00:40,536
With Spring Cloud Contract, billing
authors a contract DSL; the build
generates verification tests for the
producer and stub jars for consumers.
Gate’s tests run Feign against the stub,
not against a hope.

4
00:00:40,655 --> 00:00:49,991
Look at this example. // billing-service
contract: shouldReturnTariffQuote.groovy
Look at this java for a moment.

5
00:00:50,111 --> 00:00:59,303
Producer side: billing’s CI runs the
generated smoke tests against its
controllers — if the stub says 200 with
amount, the controller must still do that.

6
00:00:59,423 --> 00:01:10,775
Rename the JSON field without updating the
contract and producer verification fails
before merge. Consumer side: gate’s CI
downloads stubs and exercises
BillingClient.

7
00:01:10,895 --> 00:01:23,303
Breaking the contract breaks the build
before the quay feels it. Walk the Friday
rename with contracts in place: billing’s
build turns red on verification; gate
never ships a decoder that expects a
missing field.

8
00:01:23,423 --> 00:01:35,303
Contracts are not end-to-end substitutes.
They lock shapes and status codes at the
boundary. Business sequencing — check-in
then invoice — still needs integration or
journey tests.

9
00:01:35,423 --> 00:01:42,455
Prefer dedicated client DTOs on both sides
so contract fields do not drag JPA
entities across jars.

10
00:01:42,575 --> 00:01:50,351
Version stub artifacts so gate can pin a
known-good billing stub while billing
develops the next incompatible change on a
new contract file.

11
00:01:50,471 --> 00:01:59,375
Failure symptoms without contracts: Feign
DecodeException at 06:00 after a billing
deploy; each team’s green pipeline; blame
traveling across Slack.

12
00:01:59,495 --> 00:02:08,063
With contracts too loose (byRegex(".*") on
every field), the same outage returns
because nothing forced amount to stay a
money string.

13
00:02:08,183 --> 00:02:15,239
With stubs published once and never re-
verified, drift accumulates silently until
a consumer bumps the stub version.

14
00:02:15,359 --> 00:02:27,839
Trade-offs: consumer-driven contracts
(Pact-style) put gate in charge of
expectations; producer-first Contract DSL
puts billing in charge of publishing
stubs. Either works if both pipelines run.

15
00:02:27,959 --> 00:02:36,743
Contracts add build complexity; they pay
off when multiple consumers (gate,
scheduling, ops tooling) depend on
billing’s HTTP shape.

16
00:02:36,863 --> 00:02:49,655
Do not contract every admin CRUD endpoint
on day one — start with the tariff quote
path that stops trucks when it breaks.
Walk a coordinated change. Billing must
add degraded to the quote JSON.

17
00:02:49,775 --> 00:02:59,879
Update the contract body and matchers
first; regenerate stubs; fix gate’s DTO
and Feign decoder; merge producer
verification green; then consumers bump
the stub version.

18
00:02:59,999 --> 00:03:10,175
Reversing that order — ship billing first
— recreates Monday’s decoder failure with
extra ceremony. Contracts are a change
protocol, not only a test type.

19
00:03:10,295 --> 00:03:22,247
A misconception is writing contracts so
loose (byRegex(".*") everywhere) that they
never fail. Another is generating stubs
once and never re-running producer
verification.

20
00:03:22,367 --> 00:03:28,151
A third is treating contracts as
documentation only without wiring them
into both pipelines.

21
00:03:28,271 --> 00:03:39,671
Green contracts and green tariff tests
still leave a production question
unanswered: how slow is releaseGate, for
whom, since when? Phase 11 starts with
meters, not dashboards.
